# banditshell - run and drive the shell.
#
# The grammar is GENERIC-FIRST: verb, then object, then the args that define
# it. `banditshell toggle panel notifications` reads off the front; the
# bespoke tail that no generic verb can carry (a timer's start, the
# clipboard's history edits) lives behind `dispatch`, one level down.
#
#   banditshell start|stop|restart|run|log
#                                the shell itself: detach and prove it, stop,
#                                restart, foreground with the QML errors, the
#                                log
#   banditshell toggle panel <name> [screen]|border|wallpaper
#        sidebar [screen|all]|output|tablet [caller]|tablet lid [caller]
#        volume mute
#                                any surface, out or away: the fixed panels
#                                below, and every menu key (`list menu`); and
#                                the boolean states, flipped: the chrome's
#                                bare mode, the wallpaper runner, the
#                                keyboard-side column per monitor, the
#                                speakers/headphones switch, the hinge (`caller`
#                                says who asks - the compositor's switch binds
#                                pass `compositor`), mute. The level itself is
#                                a number: set, not toggle
#   banditshell open panel <name> [args]
#                                open it without reading its state first:
#                                settings takes a page then a screen, files a
#                                path, the rest a screen
#   banditshell close panel <name>
#                                put one surface away (it reaches every
#                                screen on purpose). BARE, `close` with
#                                nothing after it, shuts every surface: the
#                                rescue, one word on purpose, because a
#                                surface holding the keyboard leaves no window
#                                focused and swallows clicks. Bind it; you
#                                cannot type your way out
#   banditshell status panel <name> [screen]|border|sidebar|volume
#        |output|tablet|penmap|wallpaper|timer|alarm|lock|keyring
#        |migration
#                                what a thing is doing; bare, the shell
#                                itself
#   banditshell set border [on|off]|wallpaper [on|off]
#        sidebar [on|off] [screen|all]|tablet [on|off] [caller]
#        tablet lid [closed|open] [caller]|volume [<pct>|+n|-n]
#        volume mute [on|off]|output [speakers|headphones]
#        theme [name]|penmap rect <x> <y> <w> <h>|<key> <value>
#                                the same states, explicit, and everything
#                                that only set has: a fraction is a percent,
#                                .5 is 50, .32 is 32; +n/-n moves relative, a
#                                bare + or - one step; <key> <value> is the
#                                shell's config, dotted keys
#   banditshell get <key>|theme
#                                read one value back: a dotted config key, or
#                                the theme's name
#   banditshell list menu|clipboard|wallpaper [screen]|output|alarm|zone|themes
#                                enumerate a registry: the menu keys, the
#                                clipboard's history, the wallpapers that fit
#                                a screen, the sinks, the alarms, the places,
#                                the themes
#   banditshell dispatch timer <action>|alarm <action>|zone <action>
#        clipboard <action>|calculator <action>|keyboard <action>
#        settings <action>|launcher <action>|wallpaper <action>
#        menu <action>|penmap <action>|keyring <action>|tablet <action>
#        migration <action>|picker <action>|lock
#                                the bespoke tail, target by target. What
#                                each target answers: timer
#                                start <duration> [label]|pause|resume|toggle|
#                                cancel [handle]; alarm snooze|stop|enable|
#                                disable|remove <handle>|add <time> [--days
#                                <spec>] [--label <text>] [--run <cmd> |
#                                --ask <message>]; zone find|add|remove
#                                <place>; clipboard use|pin|remove <n>|clear;
#                                calculator answer <expression>|app|panel;
#                                keyboard page <name>|dock|float; settings
#                                page <key>|float; launcher run <id>|scrub
#                                <0..1>; wallpaper next|prev|clear [screen|
#                                all]|palette; menu current|hover; penmap
#                                commit|cancel|aspect|follow; keyring
#                                refuse|demo; tablet probe [lid]; migration
#                                migrate; picker open|freeze|clip|freezeclip|
#                                close - the ONE target that can work without
#                                the shell, falling back to grim/slurp, so
#                                the key always shoots
#   banditshell completions install|print|status|remove
#                                tab completion in zsh for every verb above,
#                                GENERATED from this comment block rather than
#                                written out a second time. `install` puts it
#                                where zsh looks and wires the fpath; after
#                                that the file rebuilds itself whenever this
#                                script is newer than it, so a verb added
#                                above needs nothing done to it. Bare, it
#                                says where it is
#   banditshell build|shaders|test
#                                compile src/*.c into bin/, recompile every
#                                components/**/*.frag to .qsb, run tests/
#                                against the plain-JavaScript parts
#   banditshell shot [file]|demo <key>|gallery [WxH]
#                                a plain screenshot; open a menu, shoot it,
#                                put it away; every component the shell owns,
#                                on buttons, with knobs
#   banditshell lockpreview [file]|settingspreview [WxH] [page] [file]
#                                the lock face and the settings face, at a
#                                size, photographed - a window on a timer,
#                                taking no input
#   banditshell filespreview [dir] [shot]
#                                the file browser in a plain window, no
#                                timeout, because a terminal you cannot type
#                                into is not a terminal
#
# Every verb above is bindable: a Hyprland bind is an exec of this CLI, and
# docs/hyprland-binds.example.conf is a ready-made set to copy or source.
#
# The block above is also DATA. scripts/zsh-completion.sh reads it to build the
# zsh completion, so its shape is a contract: two spaces, `banditshell `, the
# verb, then the alternatives separated by `|` (bracket an inner `|` to
# protect it); a description, when there is one, starts at column 31 and its
# continuation lines are indented to match. A continuation indented LESS than
# that is more alternatives (see `dispatch`).
#
# OLD BINDS still speaking the noun-first grammar (`volume set 50`,
# `wallpapers toggle`) are rewritten through services/cli-migration.js - the
# migration map - once, on the way in, and the shell's sidebar offers to
# rewrite the file itself.

set -euo pipefail

SHELL_DIR="${BANDITSHELL_DIR:-$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/.." && pwd)}"
SHOT_DIR="${BANDITSHELL_SHOTS:-${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}/banditshell}"

# Started from $HOME, ALWAYS.
#
# Every application the shell launches is its child and inherits its working
# directory, so a shell started from this repo opened every terminal in this
# repo. -p takes an absolute path, so nothing here depends on the cwd; the only
# thing it decides is where the desktop lands, and that is the user's home.
qs_() { (cd "$HOME" && qs -p "$SHELL_DIR" "$@"); }

# WHICH INSTANCE, and this is the failure it exists to stop.
#
# `qs ipc -p <dir>` finds an instance by the config path it was STARTED from,
# and the only path this script knows is the checkout it happens to live in.
# Those two agree right up until the shell is started from somewhere else,
# which a git worktree during development does as a matter of course, and from
# that moment every verb in this file addresses an instance that does not
# exist: one line to stderr, exit 1. A Hyprland bind is an exec with no
# terminal behind it, so the line goes nowhere, the KEY DOES NOTHING, and
# nothing says why. Nine binds silently dead with a shell running perfectly
# well on the screen.
#
# So the instance is the one that is RUNNING, found by asking rather than
# assumed from a path. The path stays a preference between candidates instead
# of a requirement: this checkout wins when it is up, any other shell.qml is
# taken over nothing at all. Preview instances (lockpreview.qml,
# settingspreview.qml) fall out by that filename; they are qs processes too and
# they answer no IPC.
#
# Resolved once and cached, because several verbs make more than one call.
qs_target=()
qs_target_resolved=0

list_instances() {
    qs list --all 2>/dev/null | awk '
        /^[[:space:]]*Process ID:/ { pid = $NF }
        /^[[:space:]]*Config path:/ {
            p = $0
            sub(/^[[:space:]]*Config path:[[:space:]]*/, "", p)
            print pid "\t" p
        }
    '
}

# The pid of the instance whose config is <dir>/shell.qml, if one is up.
instance_of() {
    local pid path
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r pid path; do
        [ "$path" = "$1/shell.qml" ] && { echo "$pid"; return 0; }
    done < <(list_instances)
    return 1
}

resolve_target() {
    if [ "$qs_target_resolved" = 1 ]; then
        return 0
    fi
    qs_target_resolved=1

    local pid path exact="" any=""
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r pid path; do
        case "$path" in
        "$SHELL_DIR/shell.qml") exact="$pid" ;;
        */shell.qml) [ -n "$any" ] || any="$pid" ;;
        esac
    done < <(list_instances)

    local pick="${exact:-$any}"
    if [ -n "$pick" ]; then
        qs_target=(--pid "$pick")
    else
        qs_target=(-p "$SHELL_DIR")
    fi
}

# SAY SO SOMEWHERE A KEYBIND CAN BE HEARD.
#
# stderr is the right place for a line somebody typed in a terminal and the
# wrong place for a line produced by a key. hyprctl notify draws from the
# COMPOSITOR, which is the process still standing when the shell is the thing
# that died; notify-send hands the message to the shell's own notification
# daemon, so the one failure worth announcing is the one it cannot announce.
complain() {
    echo "banditshell: $1" >&2
    hyprctl notify -1 5000 "rgb(e06c75)" "banditshell: $1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# `qs ipc call` fails loudly when the shell is not running; say so in a way that
# tells you what to do about it.
# The answer is BUFFERED rather than streamed, because qs writes its own
# failure ("No running instances for ...") to STDOUT, not to stderr. Passed
# straight through, that line lands in the middle of whatever was reading this
# verb's output as if it were the answer. So the call's output is held and
# printed only when the call actually worked.
ipc() {
    resolve_target
    local out
    if out=$(cd "$HOME" && qs ipc "${qs_target[@]}" call "$@" 2>/dev/null); then
        [ -n "$out" ] && printf '%s\n' "$out"
        return 0
    fi
    complain "not running (try: banditshell start)"
    return 1
}

# The same call for a caller that has its own answer to failure. Quiet only
# about the FAILURE: a verb that worked still says what it did, the way it
# always did from a terminal.
ipc_quiet() {
    resolve_target
    local out
    if out=$(cd "$HOME" && qs ipc "${qs_target[@]}" call "$@" 2>/dev/null); then
        [ -n "$out" ] && printf '%s\n' "$out"
        return 0
    fi
    return 1
}

# IS A SHELL RUNNING -- and not just any qs. A preview instance (gallery,
# lockpreview, settingspreview) is a qs process too, answers no IPC and is
# not the desktop, but a bare `pgrep -x qs` cannot tell the difference: it
# made `start` answer "already running" to a gallery. The test is the same
# one resolve_target asks -- a process whose config path is a shell.qml.
running() {
    qs list --all 2>/dev/null | awk '
        /^[[:space:]]*Config path:/ {
            p = $0
            sub(/^[[:space:]]*Config path:[[:space:]]*/, "", p)
            if (p ~ /\/shell\.qml$/) found = 1
        }
        END { exit found ? 0 : 1 }
    '
}

# THE LAST KNOWN GOOD COPY. A config that cannot load leaves nothing on the
# screen - no bar, no launcher, no keys - and a shell started from a keybind
# cannot even say why. So every PROVEN boot rsyncs the checkout here, and a
# boot that fails falls back to the snapshot. It is written only after a boot
# has survived the probe below, so the snapshot itself always boots.
BACKUP="${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/banditshell/lastgood"
FAILED_LOG="${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/banditshell/failed.log"

refresh_backup() {
    mkdir -p "$BACKUP"
    rsync -a --delete --exclude .git --exclude .claude --exclude .scratch \
        "$SHELL_DIR/" "$BACKUP/"
}

# LAUNCH AND PROVE IT. `qs -d` detaches before the config is parsed: a config
# that cannot load prints its errors and dies, and the exit code is 0 all the
# same - the launcher would report success over a bare desktop. So a launch is
# not believed until the instance has registered in `qs list` AND is still
# there a second later; a failed load is dead well before that. Its output,
# which a keybind launch has no terminal to show, lands in FAILED_LOG.
launch() { # launch <dir>
    local out tries=0
    out=$(mktemp)
    # Not qs_: it appends its own -p, and the dir here is an argument - the
    # snapshot has to be launchable from somewhere that is not the checkout.
    if ! (cd "$HOME" && qs -d -p "$1") >"$out" 2>&1 || grep -q "Failed to load configuration" "$out"; then
        mkdir -p "$(dirname "$FAILED_LOG")"
        cat "$out" >>"$FAILED_LOG"
        rm -f "$out"
        return 1
    fi
    while [ "$tries" -lt 30 ]; do
        if instance_of "$1" >/dev/null; then
            sleep 1
            if instance_of "$1" >/dev/null; then
                rm -f "$out"
                return 0
            fi
            break
        fi
        sleep 0.2
        tries=$((tries + 1))
    done
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$FAILED_LOG")"
    cat "$out" >>"$FAILED_LOG"
    rm -f "$out"
    return 1
}

# IF THE CHECKOUT CANNOT LOAD, THE SHOW GOES ON: the snapshot starts instead,
# and the failure is said where a keybind can be heard.
start_backup() {
    if [ ! -f "$BACKUP/shell.qml" ]; then
        complain "shell failed to load and there is no backup yet (errors: $FAILED_LOG)"
        return 1
    fi
    complain "shell failed to load - running the last known good copy (errors: $FAILED_LOG)"
    launch "$BACKUP"
}

# Print the header comment block, however long it happens to be. A hardcoded
# line range silently starts printing the code once the header changes length.
usage() { awk 'NR > 1 { if (!/^#/) exit; sub(/^# ?/, ""); print }' "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"; }

# A fraction is a percent: `.5` is 50, `.32` is 32. Whole numbers pass through
# untouched, so `set volume 50` still means 50 and the shell's own
# amplification ceiling (>100) is not clipped here.
pct_of() { awk -v v="$1" 'BEGIN{printf "%d", v * 100 + 0.5}'; }

# The relative twin: an integer is already a percent (+5 is +5%), a fraction
# is a fraction of 100 (+.5 is +50) - the same reading `set volume .5` has.
rel_of() { case "$1" in *.*) pct_of "$1" ;; *) printf '%s' "$1" ;; esac; }

# The panel nouns, and the IPC target each one drives. Most are one to one;
# the wallpaper PICKER kept its IPC target (`wallpapers`) and lost its CLI
# plural - the noun is `wallpaper`, whether it is being worn or picked.
panel_ipc() {
    case "$1" in
    wallpaper) echo wallpapers ;;
    *) echo "$1" ;;
    esac
}

# Which surfaces answer `status`, and which open with no arguments at all -
# the router refuses the verbs a target does not have rather than passing an
# arity error down to the IPC layer.
# `status` arity is not uniform: the screen-bearing panels declare the
# parameter, the rest declare none, and an empty argument to one of those is
# a "too many arguments" error rather than a quiet one.
panel_status_ok() { case "$1" in clipboard | media | calculator | keyboard | settings | notifications | notch | hotkeys | files | wallpaper | penmap) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }
panel_status_takes_screen() { case "$1" in keyboard | notifications | notch | hotkeys) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }

toggle_panel() { # <name> [screen]
    local name="$1" rest="${2:-}"
    case "$name" in
    launcher | session) ipc "$(panel_ipc "$name")" toggle ;;
    clipboard | media | calculator) ipc "$name" toggle ;;
    keyboard) ipc keyboard toggle "$rest" ;;
    settings) ipc settings toggle ;;
    notifications | notch | hotkeys) ipc "$name" toggle "$rest" ;;
    files) ipc files toggle "$rest" ;;
    wallpaper) ipc wallpapers toggle "$rest" ;;
    penmap) ipc penmap open ;;
    # Anything else is a menu KEY: the menu engine owns it.
    *) ipc menu toggle "$name" "$rest" ;;
    esac
}

open_panel() { # <name> [args...]
    local name="$1"; shift
    case "$name" in
    launcher | session) ipc "$(panel_ipc "$name")" open ;;
    clipboard | media | calculator) ipc "$name" open ;;
    keyboard) ipc keyboard open "${1:-}" ;;
    settings) ipc settings open "${1:-}" "${2:-}" ;;
    notifications | notch | hotkeys) ipc "$name" open "${1:-}" ;;
    files) ipc files open "${1:-}" ;;
    wallpaper) ipc wallpapers open "${1:-}" ;;
    penmap) ipc penmap open ;;
    *) ipc menu open "$name" "${1:-}" ;;
    esac
}

close_panel() { # <name>
    case "$1" in
    launcher | session) ipc "$(panel_ipc "$1")" close ;;
    clipboard | media | calculator | keyboard | settings | notifications | notch | hotkeys | files) ipc "$1" close ;;
    wallpaper) ipc wallpapers close ;;
    picker) ipc picker close ;;
    *) ipc menu close ;;
    esac
}

status_panel() { # <name> [screen]
    panel_status_ok "$1" || { echo "banditshell: $1 has no status" >&2; return 1; }
    if panel_status_takes_screen "$1"; then
        ipc "$(panel_ipc "$1")" status "${2:-}"
    else
        ipc "$(panel_ipc "$1")" status
    fi
}

# THE BESPOKE TAIL. `dispatch` carries the actions no generic verb can name,
# straight to the same IPC target the old grammar used - the word reorder is
# the whole change. Two exceptions keep their old intelligence: `alarm add`
# still parses its flags here (a keybind is a line somebody reads a year
# later), and `picker` still falls back to grim/slurp, because a screenshot
# key that dies with the shell is not a screenshot key.
route_dispatch() { # <target> <action> [args...]
    local target="$1"; shift
    local action="${1:-}"

    case "$target" in
    menu)
        case "$action" in
        current | hover) ipc menu "$action" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch menu current|hover" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    timer)
        case "$action" in
        pause | resume | toggle | cancel) ipc timer "$action" "${2:-}" ;;
        start) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch timer start <duration> [label]" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc timer start "$2" "${3:-}" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch timer start|pause|resume|toggle|cancel" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    alarm)
        case "$action" in
        list | status | snooze | stop) ipc alarm "$action" ;;
        enable | disable | remove) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch alarm $action <handle>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc alarm "$action" "$2" ;;
        add)
            [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch alarm add <time> [--days <spec>] [--label <text>] [--run <cmd> | --ask <message>]" >&2; exit 2; }
            local when="$2"; shift 2
            local days="" label="" mode="" payload=""
            while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
                # Every flag here takes a value, so one sitting at the end of
                # the line is a value that was forgotten rather than a flag
                # that means something alone. Checked BEFORE the shift, because
                # `shift 2` with one argument left fails, and under `set -e` a
                # failed shift ends the script with no message at all.
                [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "banditshell: $1 needs a value" >&2; exit 2; }
                case "$1" in
                --days) days="$2" ;;
                --label) label="$2" ;;
                --run) mode="command"; payload="$2" ;;
                --ask) mode="cloud"; payload="$2" ;;
                *) echo "banditshell: unknown option: $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
                esac
                shift 2
            done
            ipc alarm add "$when" "$days" "$label" "$mode" "$payload"
            ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch alarm list|status|snooze|stop|enable|disable|remove|add" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    zone)
        # The argument is EVERYTHING that follows, joined back into one string,
        # so `dispatch zone add new york` works without quotes.
        case "$action" in
        list) ipc zone list ;;
        find | add | remove) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch zone $action <place>" >&2; exit 2; }
            shift
            ipc zone "$action" "$*" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch zone list|find|add|remove" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    clipboard)
        case "$action" in
        clear) ipc clipboard clear ;;
        use | pin | remove) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch clipboard $action <index>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc clipboard "$action" "$2" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch clipboard use|pin|remove <index>|clear" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    calculator)
        case "$action" in
        app | panel) ipc calculator "$action" ;;
        answer) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch calculator answer <expression>" >&2; exit 2; }
            shift
            ipc calculator answer "$*" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch calculator app|panel|answer <expression>" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    keyboard)
        case "$action" in
        dock | float) ipc keyboard "$action" ;;
        page) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch keyboard page <name>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc keyboard page "$2" "${3:-}" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch keyboard page <name>|dock|float" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    settings)
        case "$action" in
        float) ipc settings float ;;
        page) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch settings page <key>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc settings page "$2" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch settings page <key>|float" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    launcher)
        case "$action" in
        run) ipc launcher run "${2:-}" ;;
        scrub) ipc launcher scrub "${2:-0}" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch launcher run <id>|scrub <0..1>" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    wallpaper)
        case "$action" in
        next | prev | clear) ipc wallpaper "$action" "${2:-}" ;;
        palette) ipc wallpaper palette ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch wallpaper next|prev|clear [screen|all]|palette" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    penmap)
        case "$action" in
        commit | cancel | aspect | follow) ipc penmap "$action" ;;
        probe) python3 "$SHELL_DIR/scripts/tablet-state.py" --verbose ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch penmap commit|cancel|aspect|follow" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    tablet)
        case "$action" in
        probe)
            if [ "${2:-}" = lid ]; then
                python3 "$SHELL_DIR/scripts/tablet-state.py" --lid --verbose
            else
                python3 "$SHELL_DIR/scripts/tablet-state.py" --verbose
            fi
            ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch tablet probe [lid]" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    keyring)
        case "$action" in
        refuse | demo) ipc keyring "$action" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch keyring refuse|demo" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    migration)
        case "$action" in
        migrate) ipc migration migrate ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: dispatch migration migrate" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    lock) ipc lock lock ;;

    picker)
        # THE ONE VERB THAT CAN WORK WITHOUT THE SHELL. The fallback is the
        # point: a screenshot key that does nothing is indistinguishable from
        # broken hardware. See the long note in the old grammar's picker arm -
        # the reasoning is unchanged, only the address.
        if ipc_quiet picker "$action" && [ "$action" != close ]; then
            exit 0
        fi
        case "$action" in
        close) ipc picker close; exit 0 ;;
        esac
        if ! command -v slurp >/dev/null || ! command -v grim >/dev/null; then
            complain "not running, and no grim/slurp to fall back on"
            exit 1
        fi
        complain "not running - falling back to grim/slurp (try: banditshell start)"
        region=$(slurp ${SLURP_ARGS:-}) || exit 0
        if [ -z "$region" ]; then
            exit 0
        fi
        note="banditshell was not running"
        case "$action" in
        freeze*) note="$note; taken live, nothing could be frozen" ;;
        esac
        say2() {
            notify-send -a banditshell "$1" "$2" 2>/dev/null ||
                hyprctl notify -1 5000 "rgb(e5c07b)" "$1 - $2" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
        }
        case "$action" in
        *clip)
            grim -g "$region" - | wl-copy --type image/png
            say2 "Screenshot copied" "$note"
            ;;
        *)
            cfg="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/banditshell/config.json"
            dir=$(jq -r '.picker.dir // empty' "$cfg" 2>/dev/null || true)
            editor=$(jq -r '.picker.editor // empty' "$cfg" 2>/dev/null || true)
            dir="${dir:-~/Pictures/Screenshots}"
            dir="${dir/#\~/$HOME}"
            editor="${editor:-swappy -f}"
            mkdir -p "$dir"
            out="$dir/banditshell-$(date +%Y-%m-%d-%H%M%S).png"
            grim -g "$region" "$out"
            say2 "Screenshot saved" "$out ($note)"
            # Unquoted on purpose: the configured editor is a command WITH its
            # flags ("swappy -f"), the same string the shell splits when it
            # opens one.
            # shellcheck disable=SC2086
            ${editor} "$out" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
            echo "$out"
            ;;
        esac
        ;;

    *)
        echo "banditshell: no dispatch target: $target" >&2
        exit 2
        ;;
    esac
}

# THE GENERIC ROUTE: verb, then object, then args. Booleans answer to both
# `toggle` and `set` - toggle flips, set is explicit - and a thing that is not
# on/off answers to set alone. The one-way road: everything toggleable can be
# set; not everything settable can be toggled.
route_verb() {
    local verb="$1"; shift
    local obj="${1:-}"
    if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then shift; fi

    case "$verb" in
    toggle)
        case "$obj" in
        panel) [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell toggle panel <name> [screen]" >&2; exit 2; }
            toggle_panel "$1" "${2:-}" ;;
        border) ipc border toggle ;;
        wallpaper) ipc wallpaper toggle ;;
        sidebar) ipc sidebar toggle "${1:-}" ;;
        output) ipc output toggle ;;
        tablet)
            if [ "${1:-}" = lid ]; then
                ipc tablet lid toggle "${2:-}"
            else
                ipc tablet toggle "${1:-}"
            fi ;;
        volume)
            [ "${1:-}" = mute ] || { echo "banditshell: the level is a number; use 'set volume <pct>'. Only mute toggles." >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc volume mute "" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: nothing to toggle: ${obj:-<none>} (try: toggle panel <name>|border|wallpaper|sidebar|output|tablet|volume mute)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    open)
        [ -n "$obj" ] || { echo "usage: banditshell open panel <name> [args]" >&2; exit 2; }
        case "$obj" in
        panel) [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell open panel <name> [args]" >&2; exit 2; }
            open_panel "$@" ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: only panels open: open panel <name>" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    close)
        # BARE: the rescue. One word, on purpose - see the header.
        if [ -z "$obj" ]; then
            for target in "menu close" "launcher close" "clipboard close" "session close" "hotkeys close" \
                          "settings close" "notifications close" "notch close" "picker close" \
                          "calculator close" "keyboard close" "media close"; do
                # shellcheck disable=SC2086
                ipc $target >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
            done
            echo "closed everything"
            return 0
        fi
        [ "$obj" = panel ] && [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell close panel <name> (bare 'close' shuts everything)" >&2; exit 2; }
        close_panel "$1"
        ;;

    set)
        case "$obj" in
        border) [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set border on|off" >&2; exit 2; }
            case "$1" in on | off) ipc border "$1" ;; *) echo "banditshell: set border on|off" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac ;;
        sidebar) [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set sidebar on|off [screen|all]" >&2; exit 2; }
            case "$1" in on | off) ipc sidebar "$1" "${2:-}" ;; *) echo "banditshell: set sidebar on|off [screen|all]" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac ;;
        wallpaper)
            # on|off is the runner's boolean; anything else is a PATH to wear.
            [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set wallpaper on|off|<path> [screen|all]" >&2; exit 2; }
            case "$1" in
            on | off) ipc wallpaper "$1" ;;
            "") echo "usage: banditshell set wallpaper on|off|<path> [screen|all]" >&2; exit 2 ;;
            # ABSOLUTE, because the shell is not standing in this directory
            # (see the note this inherited from the old `wallpaper set`).
            *) ipc wallpaper set "$(realpath -s -m -- "$1")" "${2:-}" ;;
            esac ;;
        volume)
            [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set volume <pct>|+n|-n|mute on|off" >&2; exit 2; }
            if [ "$1" = mute ]; then
                case "${2:-}" in
                on | off) ipc volume mute "$2" ;;
                "") echo "banditshell: set volume mute on|off (or: toggle volume mute)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
                *) echo "banditshell: set volume mute on|off" >&2; exit 2 ;;
                esac
                return 0
            fi
            case "$1" in
            # Relative: +n / -n in PERCENT (the unit `set volume 50` speaks),
            # a fraction again a fraction of 100 (+.5 is +50), and the bare
            # sign means one step of the shell's own size - the old `volume
            # up` / `volume down`.
            +*) n="${1#+}"; if [ -z "$n" ]; then ipc volume up ""; else ipc volume upPercent "$(rel_of "$n")"; fi ;;
            -*) n="${1#-}"; if [ -z "$n" ]; then ipc volume down ""; else ipc volume downPercent "$(rel_of "$n")"; fi ;;
            # A fraction is a percent.
            *.*) ipc volume set "$(pct_of "$1")" ;;
            *) ipc volume set "$1" ;;
            esac ;;
        output)
            [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set output speakers|headphones|<role> <name>" >&2; exit 2; }
            case "$1" in
            speakers | headphones) ipc output "$1" ;;
            "") echo "usage: banditshell set output speakers|headphones|<role> <name>" >&2; exit 2 ;;
            # An empty name is a real argument: it unassigns the role.
            *) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set output <role> <name>" >&2; exit 2; }
                ipc output assign "$1" "${2:-}" ;;
            esac ;;
        theme) [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set theme <name>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc shell set theme "\"$1\"" ;;
        penmap)
            [ "$1" = rect ] && [ $# -ge 5 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set penmap rect <x> <y> <w> <h>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc penmap set "$2" "$3" "$4" "$5" ;;
        # Anything else is a dotted config key.
        "") echo "usage: banditshell set <key> <value>" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        *) [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell set <key> <value>" >&2; exit 2; }
            ipc shell set "$obj" "$2" ;;
        esac
        ;;

    get)
        case "$obj" in
        "") echo "usage: banditshell get <key>|theme" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        theme) ipc shell get theme ;;
        *) ipc shell get "$obj" ;;
        esac
        ;;

    list)
        case "$obj" in
        menu) ipc menu list ;;
        clipboard) ipc clipboard list ;;
        wallpaper) ipc wallpaper list "${2:-}" ;;
        output) ipc output list ;;
        alarm) ipc alarm list ;;
        zone) ipc zone list ;;
        themes) ipc shell themes ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: list menu|clipboard|wallpaper|output|alarm|zone|themes" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;

    status)
        case "$obj" in
        "") ipc shell status ;;
        panel) [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell status panel <name> [screen]" >&2; exit 2; }
            status_panel "$1" "${2:-}" ;;
        border | sidebar | volume | output | tablet | penmap | wallpaper | timer | alarm | lock | keyring | migration) ipc "$obj" status ;;
        *) echo "banditshell: no status for: $obj" >&2; exit 2 ;;
        esac
        ;;
    esac
}

cmd="${1:-help}"
shift || true

case "$cmd" in
start)
    running && { echo "already running"; exit 0; }
    if launch "$SHELL_DIR"; then
        refresh_backup
        echo "started"
    else
        start_backup || exit 1
        echo "running from backup - the checkout itself failed to load (errors: $FAILED_LOG)" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    ;;
stop)
    # -x, not -f: `pkill -f qs` matches this script's own command line and kills
    # the terminal it was typed in.
    pkill -x qs || true
    echo "stopped"
    ;;
restart)
    pkill -x qs || true
    # POLL for the death rather than hoping 0.5s was enough: the new instance
    # needs the old one's IPC name gone, and on a busy machine the old one can
    # still be dying a second in. Give up waiting after two seconds and start
    # anyway -- if the name is still taken, qs says so itself.
    for _ in $(seq 1 40); do
        pgrep -x qs >/dev/null 2>&1 || break
        sleep 0.05
    done
    if launch "$SHELL_DIR"; then
        refresh_backup
        echo "restarted"
    else
        start_backup || exit 1
        echo "running from backup - the checkout itself failed to load (errors: $FAILED_LOG)" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    ;;
run)
    # Foreground, so QML errors land in the terminal. Spelled out rather than
    # calling qs_: bash's exec replaces the process from PATH and cannot run a
    # shell function, so `exec qs_` dies with "not found" before qs is reached.
    cd "$HOME" || exit 1
    exec qs -p "$SHELL_DIR"
    ;;
log)
    exec qs -p "$SHELL_DIR" log "$@"
    ;;

# THE GENERIC VERBS, routed. Every one of these used to be a noun-first arm
# with its own bespoke case; the noun is now the OBJECT, the verb is the
# command, and the wiring lives in route_verb / route_dispatch above.
toggle|open|set|get|list|status)
    route_verb "$cmd" "$@"
    ;;

close)
    # Bare, the rescue - one word, because the moment it is needed typing is
    # exactly what has already failed. With `panel <name>`, the surface's own
    # close.
    route_verb close "$@"
    ;;

panic)
    route_verb close
    ;;

dispatch)
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell dispatch <target> <action> [args...]" >&2; exit 2; }
    route_dispatch "$@"
    ;;

# THE OLD GRAMMAR, on the way in. A bind written before the overhaul
# (`volume set 50`, `wallpapers toggle`, `calendar`) is not dead: it is
# rewritten ONCE through the migration map - services/cli-migration.js, the
# single authority on old -> new - and this script re-enters with the new
# words. node only pays for the old forms, which fade as the map's rewriter
completions)
    sub="${1:-status}"
    shift || true
    case "$sub" in
    install | print | status | remove)
        exec "$SHELL_DIR/scripts/zsh-completion.sh" "$sub" "$@"
        ;;
    *) echo "usage: banditshell completions install|print|status|remove" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    ;;

shot)
    mkdir -p "$SHOT_DIR"
    out="${1:-$SHOT_DIR/shot.png}"
    grim "$out"
    echo "$out"
    ;;

files)
    sub="${1:-toggle}"
    shift || true
    case "$sub" in
    # Only the two that OPEN take a path. `close` and `status` declare no
    # parameter, and an extra argument is a hard error on the other side rather
    # than an ignored one, so passing "" to them turned both into failures.
    toggle | open) ipc files "$sub" "${1:-}" ;;
    close | status) ipc files "$sub" ;;
    *) echo "usage: banditshell files toggle|open [path]|close|status" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    ;;

# The C helpers, compiled here because Quickshell loads QML out of a directory
# and has nowhere to run a build step - the same reason `shaders` exists, and the
# same bargain: the binary is build output that lives next to its source.
#
# Not a Makefile. Two translation units with no options between them do not need
# one, and a build system would be a second thing to install before the shell
# runs at all.
build)
    cc="${CC:-gcc}"
    command -v "$cc" >/dev/null || { echo "banditshell: no C compiler (set \$CC, or install gcc)" >&2; exit 1; }
    mkdir -p "$SHELL_DIR/bin"
    # bs-pty wants -lutil for forkpty; bs-ls wants nothing. Listed per target
    # rather than shared, because a link line that is one thing for everything
    # is a link line nobody reads.
    "$cc" -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o "$SHELL_DIR/bin/bs-pty" "$SHELL_DIR/src/bs-pty.c" -lutil
    echo "src/bs-pty.c -> bin/bs-pty"
    "$cc" -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o "$SHELL_DIR/bin/bs-ls" "$SHELL_DIR/src/bs-ls.c"
    echo "src/bs-ls.c -> bin/bs-ls"
    ;;

shaders)
    # .qsb files are build output committed alongside their source, because
    # Quickshell loads QML from a directory and has nowhere to run a build step.
    # This is that build step; run it after editing any .frag.
    qsb="${QSB:-/usr/lib/qt6/bin/qsb}"
    [ -x "$qsb" ] || { echo "banditshell: qsb not found (install qt6-shadertools)" >&2; exit 1; }
    # EVERY .frag under components/, wherever it sits. It used to be the one
    # directory the first shader happened to live in, so the second one was
    # written, committed, and silently never compiled: the shell loaded the
    # stale .qsb that was not there and drew nothing at all.
    while IFS= read -r f; do
        "$qsb" --glsl "100es,120,150" --hlsl 50 --msl 12 -o "$f.qsb" "$f"
        echo "${f#"$SHELL_DIR/"} -> ${f#"$SHELL_DIR/"}.qsb"
    done < <(find "$SHELL_DIR/components" -name '*.frag' | sort)
    ;;

test)
    # The parts of the shell that are plain JavaScript are the parts a
    # screenshot cannot check. A colour that resolves to the wrong hex, an
    # erase that forgets its background, a character split across two reads of
    # the pty: none of those throw, they just make the terminal quietly wrong,
    # and the only evidence is a picture that looks a bit off. Node runs the
    # same files with no Qt under them, which is the whole reason vt.js has no
    # QML in it.
    #
    # A glob rather than the directory: node 26 reads `--test tests/` as a
    # module to load and fails with "Cannot find module". Quoted so node does
    # the expanding, since it is node's idea of a test file that decides.
    command -v node >/dev/null || { echo "banditshell: node not found" >&2; exit 1; }
    cd "$SHELL_DIR" || exit 1
    exec node --test 'tests/*.test.js' "$@"
    ;;

demo)
    # Open a menu, wait for the reveal to settle, capture it, put it away. The
    # sleep is the animation, not a guess: the reveal is exponential smoothing,
    # so it is asymptotic and needs a moment rather than a frame.
    [ $# -ge 1 ] || { echo "usage: banditshell demo <key>" >&2; exit 2; }
    mkdir -p "$SHOT_DIR"
    out="${2:-$SHOT_DIR/menu-$1.png}"
    ipc menu open "$1" "" >/dev/null
    sleep 0.6
    grim "$out"
    ipc menu close >/dev/null
    echo "$out"
    ;;

filespreview)
    # The browser in a plain window, for looking at it without touching the
    # running shell's own session. lockpreview's shape, with one difference: no
    # timeout, because a terminal you cannot type into for more than nine
    # seconds is not a terminal you can test. Close the window to end it.
    #
    # `banditshell filespreview [dir] [shot]` - with a second argument it
    # screenshots the window's own rect and exits, which is the form worth
    # binding while working on the layout.
    where="${1:-$HOME}"
    out="${2:-}"
    # Floated and sized by a window rule, the way settingspreview does it: a
    # tiled preview is whatever size the layout gives it, and a file grid at
    # 2500px wide is not the grid anybody will actually use.
    w=$(ipc shell get files.width 2>/dev/null || echo 1200)
    h=$(ipc shell get files.height 2>/dev/null || echo 760)
    hyprctl eval "hl.window_rule({ name = \"banditshell-filespreview\", match = { title = \"^(banditshell-filespreview)\$\" }, float = true, size = { $w, $h }, center = true, no_anim = true })" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
        hyprctl --batch "keyword windowrule float on, match:title ^(banditshell-filespreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule size $w $h, match:title ^(banditshell-filespreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule center on, match:title ^(banditshell-filespreview)\$" >/dev/null 2>&1
    (cd "$HOME" && FILES_PREVIEW="$where" qs -p "$SHELL_DIR/filespreview.qml" >/dev/null 2>&1) &
    preview=$!
    if [ -n "$out" ]; then
        mkdir -p "$SHOT_DIR"
        sleep 4
        rect=$(hyprctl -j clients | jq -r '.[] | select(.title == "banditshell-filespreview") | "\(.at[0]),\(.at[1]) \(.size[0])x\(.size[1])"' | head -1)
        if [ -n "$rect" ]; then grim -g "$rect" "$out"; else grim "$out"; fi
        kill "$preview" 2>/dev/null
        echo "$out"
    else
        wait "$preview"
    fi
    ;;

lockpreview)
    # The lock screen's LOOK, without the lock. A session lock cannot be opened
    # to look at, and the way back from one that drew wrong is a TTY, so the face
    # is a plain Item that also fits in an ordinary overlay surface.
    #
    # Its own quickshell instance, on a timer, taking no input: it draws over
    # everything for a few seconds and then goes away on its own whatever
    # happens. Deliberately NOT killed with pkill afterwards, which would take
    # the running shell with it.
    mkdir -p "$SHOT_DIR"
    out="${1:-$SHOT_DIR/lock.png}"
    (cd "$HOME" && timeout 9 qs -p "$SHELL_DIR/lockpreview.qml" >/dev/null 2>&1) &
    sleep 4
    grim "$out"
    wait
    echo "$out"
    ;;

settingspreview)
    # The settings face at a SIZE, which is the thing a running shell cannot
    # show you: its card is one size, and the page has three shapes. A plain
    # floating window of the asked-for size draws the same face, and grim
    # shoots that window's rect alone so the picture is the page and not the
    # desktop it happens to be over. Its own quickshell instance, on a timer,
    # for lockpreview's reason.
    mkdir -p "$SHOT_DIR"
    size="${1:-560x560}"
    page="${2:-}"
    out="${3:-$SHOT_DIR/settings-$size${page:+-$page}.png}"
    # FLOATED AND SIZED BY A WINDOW RULE, installed before the window exists:
    # a tiled window is whatever size the layout gives it, and the size IS the
    # question being asked. The same rule services/Settings.qml installs for
    # the real settings window, in whichever dialect this Hyprland speaks (the
    # Lua parser refuses `keyword`, the legacy one has no `eval`).
    #
    # AND OUT OF THE WAY: on a workspace of its own, silently, and without
    # taking the keyboard. A preview is spawned to be photographed, not
    # looked at, and one that landed on the workspace being worked in and
    # stole focus would interrupt exactly the person who asked for it.
    # PREVIEW_WORKSPACE names the workspace; the default is the first band of
    # the second screen (the vertical monitor here).
    w="${size%x*}"; h="${size#*x}"
    ws="${PREVIEW_WORKSPACE:-6}"
    hyprctl eval "hl.window_rule({ name = \"banditshell-settingspreview\", match = { title = \"^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$\" }, float = true, size = { $w, $h }, center = true, no_anim = true, workspace = \"$ws silent\", no_initial_focus = true, no_focus = true })" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
        hyprctl --batch "keyword windowrule float on, match:title ^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule size $w $h, match:title ^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule center on, match:title ^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule workspace $ws silent, match:title ^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule no_initial_focus on, match:title ^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$ ; keyword windowrule no_focus on, match:title ^(banditshell-settingspreview)\$" >/dev/null 2>&1
    (cd "$HOME" && SETTINGS_PREVIEW="$size" SETTINGS_PAGE="$page" timeout 9 qs -p "$SHELL_DIR/settingspreview.qml" >/dev/null 2>&1) &
    sleep 4
    rect=$(hyprctl -j clients | jq -r '.[] | select(.title == "banditshell-settingspreview") | "\(.at[0]),\(.at[1]) \(.size[0])x\(.size[1])"' | head -1)
    if [ -n "$rect" ]; then grim -g "$rect" "$out"; else grim "$out"; fi
    wait
    echo "$out"
    ;;

gallery)
    # THE WORKBENCH. settingspreview's shape with the opposite purpose: the
    # previews exist to be photographed and killed, so they run on a timeout
    # and refuse focus. The gallery exists to be USED - flipped, dragged,
    # compared - so it takes focus, sets no timeout, and stays up until it
    # is closed. Same window rule as the previews, minus the parts that push
    # it out of the way: floated, sized, centred, before the window exists.
    #
    # SELF-HEALING, because things have already gone wrong here: a reload
    # that died mid-edit could leave a WINDOWLESS qs husk squatting on the
    # config path; a second run stacked a second window; and a window on
    # another workspace looked exactly like "the command did nothing". The
    # gallery now QUITS on window close (gallery.qml), so husks cannot
    # accumulate any more; the sweep stays as the janitor for anything older
    # than that fix, and every run of this command starts a fresh gallery -
    # more than one is allowed, drag them apart.
    size="${1:-1280x800}"
    w="${size%x*}"; h="${size#*x}"
    for pid in $(pgrep -f "^qs -p $SHELL_DIR/gallery" 2>/dev/null); do
        kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null && echo "windowless husk $pid put down"
    done
    hyprctl eval "hl.window_rule({ name = \"banditshell-gallery\", match = { title = \"^(banditshell-gallery)\$\" }, float = true, size = { $w, $h }, center = true, no_anim = true })" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
        hyprctl --batch "keyword windowrule float on, match:title ^(banditshell-gallery)\$ ; keyword windowrule size $w $h, match:title ^(banditshell-gallery)\$ ; keyword windowrule center on, match:title ^(banditshell-gallery)\$" >/dev/null 2>&1
    (cd "$HOME" && GALLERY_SIZE="$size" qs -p "$SHELL_DIR/gallery.qml" >/dev/null 2>&1) &
    echo "gallery up at $size"
    ;;

help | -h | --help) usage ;;
# THE OLD GRAMMAR, on the way in. A bind written before the overhaul
# (`volume set 50`, `wallpapers toggle`, `calendar`) is not dead: it is
# rewritten ONCE through the migration map - services/cli-migration.js, the
# single authority on old -> new - and this script re-enters with the new
# words. node only pays for the old forms, which fade as the map's rewriter
# reaches the files they live in.
*)
    old_args="$cmd${*:+ $*}"
    if command -v node >/dev/null \
        && new_args=$(node "$SHELL_DIR/services/cli-migration.js" --cmd "$old_args" 2>/dev/null) \
        && [ -n "$new_args" ]; then
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        exec "$BASH_SOURCE" $new_args
    fi
    usage >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac
